From u778abaa663647a33 at groups.google.com  Thu May  9 14:08:39 2013
From: u778abaa663647a33 at groups.google.com (Jeff Laake - NOAA Federal)
Date: Thu, 09 May 2013 14:08:39 +0000
Subject: Re: Estimating g(0) for passive acoustic surveys
Message-ID: <wcelbm2agqI.qxjVHiiy4YoJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <wcelbm2agqI.zmcvrWHh1jUJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <wcelbm2agqI.zmcvrWHh1jUJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/wcelbm2agqI/m/qxjVHiiy4YoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It could be too low if there is negative dependence between visual and
acoustic detections which is very likely.  Negative dependence means what
can be heard can't be seen and what can be seen can't be heard to some
degree.  Typically with methods using dual visual observers the dependence
is positive which can mean g(0) estimate is too high. But with different
detection techniques the dependence can be negative.  I believe this is
written up in a report on acoustic methods for marine mammal surveys that I
attended a number of years ago in San Diego.

regards --jeff

